{"cuisine/new-american-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/new-american-cuisine","n":"New American cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["New American cuisine, also known as Modern American cuisine or Contemporary American cuisine, is the wave of modernized cooking predominantly served at upscale fine dining restaurants in the United States, originating in the 1980s. New American cuisine is generally a type of fusion cuisine which assimilates flavors from the melting pot of traditional American cooking techniques mixed with foreign and sometimes molecular gastronomy components. There is often a focus on fresh, local, and seasonal farm-to-table ingredients."],["The movement was popularized in the 1990s by various celebrity chefs, including Wolfgang Puck, Jeremiah Tower, Alice Waters, and Jonathan Waxman."],["New American cuisine features innovative use of seasoning and sauces. Originally based on French, nouvelle, and traditional American cuisine, New American has since progressed to include elements of Mediterranean, Latin American, Asian, and other cuisines."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"new-american-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_American_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/69/Motherhood_and_apple_pie.jpg/500px-Motherhood_and_apple_pie.jpg","w":500,"h":334,"by":"Scott Bauer, USDA ARS","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Motherhood_and_apple_pie.jpg"}},"cuisine/bukharan-jewish-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/bukharan-jewish-cuisine","n":"Bukharan Jewish cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Cuisine of a people","intro":[["Bukharan Jewish cuisine is the traditional cuisine originating from the Bukharian Jewish community of Central Asia, who now mostly reside in Israel and the United States."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bukharan_Jewish_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Jewish Food Society: Bukharian and Central Asian Jewish recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"jewishfoodsociety.org","note":"Nonprofit archive of family recipes, with a Bukharian collection.","url":"https://www.jewishfoodsociety.org/collections/bukharian-central-asian-jewish-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2a/Krautwickerl_mit_Wirsing_und_Kartoffel_3.jpg/500px-Krautwickerl_mit_Wirsing_und_Kartoffel_3.jpg","w":500,"h":314,"by":"Burkhard Mücke","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krautwickerl_mit_Wirsing_und_Kartoffel_3.jpg","of":"Cabbage roll"}},"cuisine/itameshi":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/itameshi","n":"Itameshi","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Japan","intro":[["Itameshi is a type of fusion cuisine originally from Japan that combines traditional elements of Japanese food and Italian food. The name comes from the combination of the Japanese name for Italy (イタリア Itaria) and the Japanese word for meal (飯, meshi). This term is used to differentiate Japanese-Italian fusion food from standard Italian food, which is formally referred to in Japanese as itaria-ryōri (イタリア料理)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"itameshi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Itameshi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Just One Cookbook's roundup of Japanese-style pasta recipes.","url":"https://www.justonecookbook.com/popular-japanese-wafu-pasta-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Sudachi Recipes: Wafu Pasta","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"sudachirecipes.com","note":"Japanese-style pasta recipes from Japanese cook Yuto Omura.","url":"https://sudachirecipes.com/wafu-pasta-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/58/Spaghetti_TARAKO_Sauce%2C_at_Saizeriya.jpg/500px-Spaghetti_TARAKO_Sauce%2C_at_Saizeriya.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Lombroso","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spaghetti_TARAKO_Sauce%2C_at_Saizeriya.jpg"}},"cuisine/south-karnataka":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/south-karnataka","n":"Dakshina Karnataka cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["South Karnataka generally refers to the southern part of Karnataka state, excluding the coastal areas. It generally corresponds to former Mysore state. It is generally referred as Hale Mysuru Region in Kannada which translates to Old Mysore Region or OMR. Kannada dialect of South Karnataka is slightly different as compared to North Karnataka."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"south-karnataka"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Karnataka"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/da/India_south_karnataka_district.jpg/500px-India_south_karnataka_district.jpg","w":500,"h":723,"by":"Raghu Naik NC","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:India_south_karnataka_district.jpg"}},"cuisine/purepecha-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/purepecha-foodways","n":"Purépecha foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Lake Pátzcuaro, the Meseta Purépecha and the Cañada de los Once Pueblos, Michoacán, Mexico","names":"Purépecha","intro":[["When UNESCO recognized traditional Mexican cuisine, it named Michoacán as the model: a way of eating built on ",["corn","ingredient/maize"],", ",["beans","ingredient/beans"]," and ",["chile","ingredient/chili-pepper"],", grown in the milpa, made into dough by ",["Nixtamalization","ingredient/nixtamalization"]," and ground on stone, and kept alive by collectives of women cooks. Two cocineras from the region, Antonina González Leandro of Tarerio and Juanita Bravo Lázaro of Angahuan, traveled to Nairobi to receive it."],["Around Lake Pátzcuaro and up on the Meseta Purépecha, cocineras tradicionales keep the dishes going. Corundas, a kind of ",["tamal","dish/tamale"],", come plain or with ash, chard or cheese; uchepos of fresh corn come with cream and salsa; churipo is served with plain corundas; atápakua is made with nopal or charales, small lake fish; and there are atoles of every kind. Each year they cook, teach and compete at the Encuentro de Cocina Tradicional, Michoacán's largest food festival."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"UNESCO, Traditional Mexican cuisine, the Michoacán paradigm (decision 5.COM 6.30)","url":"https://ich.unesco.org/en/decisions/5.COM/6.30"},{"name":"México Desconocido, Ruta de cocineras tradicionales, de Santa Fe de la Laguna a Tarecuato","url":"https://www.mexicodesconocido.com.mx/ruta-de-cocineras-tradicionales-de-santa-fe-de-la-laguna-tarecuato.html"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"purepecha-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Tamale","dish/tamale"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Banana leaf","ingredient/banana-leaf"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Corn husks","ingredient/corn-husks"],["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Masa","ingredient/masa"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a5/Tamale_Oaxaque%C3%B1o.jpg/500px-Tamale_Oaxaque%C3%B1o.jpg","w":500,"h":382,"by":"Nsaum75","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tamale_Oaxaque%C3%B1o.jpg","of":"Tamale"}},"ingredient/radish":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/radish","n":"Radish","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["The radish (Raphanus sativus or Raphanus raphanistrum subsp. sativus) is a flowering plant in the mustard family, Brassicaceae. Its large taproot is commonly used as a root vegetable, although the entire plant is edible and its leaves are sometimes used as a leaf vegetable. Domesticated in Asia, radishes are grown and consumed globally. The radish is sometimes considered to form a species complex with the wild radish and instead treated as the subspecies Raphanus raphanistrum subsp. sativus."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-radish"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radish"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Kazakh cuisine","cuisine/kazakh-cuisine",[["Shalgam","dish/shalgam"]]],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Boat noodles","dish/boat-noodles"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bean sprouts","ingredient/bean-sprouts"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Dark soy sauce","ingredient/dark-soy-sauce"],["Fermented bean curd","ingredient/fermented-bean-curd"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Morning glory","ingredient/morning-glory"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Pickled bean curd","ingredient/pickled-bean-curd"],["Pig blood","ingredient/pig-blood"],["Rice noodles","ingredient/rice-noodles"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/0c/Radish_3371103037_4ab07db0bf_o.jpg/500px-Radish_3371103037_4ab07db0bf_o.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Self, en:User:Jengod","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Radish_3371103037_4ab07db0bf_o.jpg"}},"ingredient/oxtail":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/oxtail","n":"Oxtail","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Oxtail (occasionally spelled ox tail or ox-tail) is the culinary name for the tail of cattle. While the word once meant only the tail of an ox, today it can also refer to the tails of other cattle. An oxtail typically weighs around 3.5 kilograms (8 pounds) and is skinned and cut into shorter lengths for sale."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-oxtail"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxtail"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Coda alla vaccinara","dish/coda-alla-vaccinara"]]],["Lazian or Roman","cuisine/roman-cuisine",[["Coda alla vaccinara","dish/coda-alla-vaccinara"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/94/Raw_oxtail-01.jpg/500px-Raw_oxtail-01.jpg","w":500,"h":293,"by":"FotoosVanRobin","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raw_oxtail-01.jpg"}},"dish/doenjang":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/doenjang","n":"Doenjang","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Korean cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Doenjang or soybean paste is a type of fermented bean paste made of soybean and brine used in Korean cuisine. It is also a byproduct of soup soy sauce production. It is sometimes used as a relish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-doenjang"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doenjang"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Brine","ingredient/brine"],["Meju","ingredient/meju"],["Soybean","ingredient/soybean"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Maangchi","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"maangchi.com","note":"Maangchi's Korean recipe site and YouTube channel, one of the best known English sources for Korean cooking.","url":"https://www.maangchi.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Korean Bapsang","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Korean cuisine","links":[["Bap (rice dish)","dish/bap-rice-dish"],["Bibim-guksu","dish/bibim-guksu"],["Bibimbap","dish/bibimbap"],["Bosintang","dish/bosintang"],["Cheonggukjang","dish/cheonggukjang"],["Garak-guksu","dish/gara