on regional pasta dishes from Abruzzo.","url":"https://www.the-pasta-project.com/abruzzo/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Abruzzese and Molisan cuisine","links":[["Arrosticini","dish/arrosticini"],["Aurum (liqueur)","dish/aurum-liqueur"],["Brodetto alla vastese","dish/brodetto-alla-vastese"],["Centerbe","dish/centerbe"],["Confetti di Sulmona","dish/confetti-di-sulmona"],["Mortadella di Campotosto","dish/mortadella-di-campotosto"],["Pecora alla callara","dish/pecora-alla-callara"],["Torrone Nurzia","dish/torrone-nurzia"],["Vino cotto","dish/vino-cotto"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e2/Fiadone.jpg/500px-Fiadone.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Posotivoli","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fiadone.jpg"}},"ingredient/apples":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/apples","n":"Apples","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["An apple is the round, edible fruit of an apple tree (Malus spp.). Fruit trees of the orchard or domestic apple (Malus domestica), the most widely grown in the genus, are cultivated worldwide. The tree originated in Central Asia, where its wild ancestor, Malus sieversii, is still found. Apples have been grown for thousands of years in Eurasia before they were introduced to the American continent by European colonists. Apples have cultural significance in many mythologies (including Norse and Greek) and religions (such as Christianity in Europe)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-apples"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apples"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Jammie Dodgers","dish/jammie-dodgers"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Apples","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":95053485,"top":[["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",47701620],["United States of America","cuisine/american-cuisine",4777056],["Turkey","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",4526790],["Poland","cuisine/polish-cuisine",3832900],["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",2636180],["Italy","cuisine/italian-cuisine",2319342],["Iran","cuisine/iranian-cuisine",2302884],["Russia","cuisine/russian-cuisine",2147305],["France","cuisine/french-cuisine",1804114],["Chile","cuisine/chilean-cuisine",1491285],["Uzbekistan","cuisine/uzbek-cuisine",1313616],["South Africa","cuisine/south-african-cuisine",1181173],["Ukraine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",1169242],["Brazil","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",1101832],["Germany","cuisine/german-cuisine",982426]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Malic acid","ingredient/malic-acid"],["Raspberries","ingredient/raspberries"],["Raspberry jam","ingredient/raspberry-jam"],["Shortbread","ingredient/shortbread"],["Sodium bicarbonate","ingredient/sodium-bicarbonate"],["Strawberries","ingredient/strawberries"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tartaric acid","ingredient/tartaric-acid"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}]},"ingredient/cottage-cheese":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cottage-cheese","n":"Cottage cheese","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 6 cuisines","intro":[["Cottage cheese is a type of fresh cheese with a mild flavor and a creamy, heterogeneous texture made from skimmed milk – hence the low milkfat content. It is made by adding an acid – like vinegar or lemon juice – to the milk which causes the milk proteins (curds) to separate from the liquid (whey). The curds are then drained and mixed with cream and salt, giving cottage cheese its slightly tangy taste and creamy texture. Cottage cheese is not aged."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cottage-cheese"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cottage_cheese"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":6,"groups":[["Austrian cuisine","cuisine/austrian-cuisine",[["Liptauer","dish/liptauer"]]],["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine",[["Syrniki","dish/syrniki"]]],["Hungarian cuisine","cuisine/hungarian-cuisine",[["Liptauer","dish/liptauer"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Syrniki","dish/syrniki"]]],["Slovak cuisine","cuisine/slovak-cuisine",[["Liptauer","dish/liptauer"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Syrniki","dish/syrniki"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bryndza","ingredient/bryndza"],["Goat cheese","ingredient/goat-cheese"],["Quark","ingredient/quark"],["Quark cheese","ingredient/quark-cheese"],["Sheep milk","ingredient/sheep-milk"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Quark (dairy product)","ingredient/quark-dairy-product"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tvorog","ingredient/tvorog"],["Vanilla extract","ingredient/vanilla-extract"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/16/Cottagecheese200px.jpg/500px-Cottagecheese200px.jpg","w":500,"h":398,"by":"FASTILY (TALK)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cottagecheese200px.jpg"}},"dish/panipuri":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/panipuri","n":"Panipuri","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Panipuri (also known by other names, including phuchka and golgappa) is a snack associated with the cuisines of the Indian subcontinent consisting of a deep-fried spherical puri shell, hollowed out for a filling and dipped in flavoured waters. Panipuri is primarily a street food and is part of the chaat category of light snacks. It is commonly filled with some combination of potatoes, chickpeas, spices, and chutney. The flavoured waters, or pani, are typically a spicy coriander leaf or mint chutney called teekha pani and a sweet tamarind chutney called meetha pani. A few centimetres in diameter, it is a finger food eaten in one bite. Panipuri is the most common street food in the Indian subcontinent, and it is popular across the region, in both urban and rural areas."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-panipuri"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panipuri"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":8,"links":[["Atta (flour)","ingredient/atta-flour"],["Chaat masala","ingredient/chaat-masala"],["Chickpea","ingredient/chickpea"],["Coriander leaf","ingredient/coriander-leaf"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"],["Tamarind","ingredient/tamarind"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Swasthi's Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"indianhealthyrecipes.com","note":"Swasthi Shreekanth's long-running blog of everyday Indian home cooking from across the regions.","url":"https://www.indianhealthyrecipes.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Hari Ghotra","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"harighotra.co.uk","note":"UK-based cook Hari Ghotra's recipe library and videos of home-style Indian dishes.","url":"https://www.harighotra.co.uk/indian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Indian cuisine","links":[["Akuri","dish/akuri"],["Aloo paratha","dish/aloo-paratha"],["Amba (condiment)","dish/amba-condiment"],["Baingan bharta","dish/baingan-bharta"],["Bombay potato","dish/bombay-potato"],["Butter chicken","dish/butter-chicken"],["Chaat masala","dish/chaat-masala"],["Chicken Chettinad","dish/chicken-chettinad"],["Dahi vada","dish/dahi-vada"],["Dal dhokli","dish/dal-dhokli"],["Dal makhani","dish/dal-makhani"],["Fafda","dish/fafda"],["Falooda","dish/falooda"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Handvo","dish/handvo"],["Hyderabadi haleem","dish/hyderabadi-haleem"],["Idli","dish/idli"],["Indian restaurant","dish/i