am goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Cenil","dish/cenil"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Cimpa","dish/cimpa"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dadiah","dish/dadiah"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b3/10._Sate_Klathak_2.jpg/500px-10._Sate_Klathak_2.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Danangtrihartanto","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:10._Sate_Klathak_2.jpg"}},"dish/tajine-lham-lahlou":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/tajine-lham-lahlou","n":"Tajine lham-lahlou","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#e46a3f","intro":[["Lham hlou also known as lham lahlou and tajine lahlou. which means \"sweet meat\" or \"sweet tajne\", is an Algerian sweet dish made with meat and mainly prunes, possibly with apricots and decorated with raisins and almonds in a syrup of sugar and orange blossom water. Apples are usually used as well. The meat and vegetables are first sauteed with onions and smen (traditional preserved North African butter). This dish is served as a starter or as a dessert during Ramadan and on the occasion of wedding celebrations."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-tajine-lham-lahlou"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tajine_lham-lahlou"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Algerian cuisine","cuisine/algerian-cuisine"],["Moroccan cuisine","cuisine/moroccan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Apricots","ingredient/apricots"],["Prunes","ingredient/prunes"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Amour de Cuisine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"amourdecuisine.fr","note":"Algerian home cooking and pastries from Algerian born cook Soulef Zaoui, in English and French.","url":"https://www.amourdecuisine.fr/en","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"My Moroccan Food","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mymoroccanfood.com","note":"Moroccan recipe blog linked to cookbook author Nargisse Benkabbou, covering breads, soups and tagines.","url":"https://www.mymoroccanfood.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Taste of Maroc","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"tasteofmaroc.com","note":"Moroccan recipes and tutorials from Christine Benlafquih, formerly the Moroccan food writer at The Spruce Eats.","url":"https://tasteofmaroc.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"},{"name":"Salima's Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"salimaskitchen.com","note":"Moroccan section of Salima Benkhalti's blog, drawing on her family cooking.","url":"https://salimaskitchen.com/category/moroccan/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Algerian cuisine","links":[["Couscous","dish/couscous"],["Karantika","dish/karantika"],["Kourdass","dish/kourdass"],["Mbesses","dish/mbesses"],["Mhancha","dish/mhancha"],["Zviti","dish/zviti"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/70/Lham_lahlou_algerien.jpg/500px-Lham_lahlou_algerien.jpg","w":500,"h":613,"by":"Slothtysloth","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lham_lahlou_algerien.jpg"}},"dish/taqueria":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/taqueria","n":"Taquería","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Mexican cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A taco stand,  taco shop or taquería is a food stall, food cart or restaurant that specializes in tacos and other Mexican dishes. The food is typically prepared quickly and tends to be inexpensive. Many various ingredients may be used, and various taco styles may be served. Taco stands are an integral part of Mexican street food. Tacos became a part of traditional Mexican cuisine in the early 20th century, beginning in Mexico City, as what had been a miner's snack began to be sold on street corners in the city. Shops selling tacos have since proliferated throughout Mexico and other areas with a heavy Mexican culinary and cultural influence, including much of the Western United States and most other larger American cities. More typical taquerías specialize in tacos, as expected, but in some localities it can be used to refer to restaurants specializing in burritos, where tacos themselves"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-taqueria"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taquer%C3%ADa"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Mexico in My Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mexicoinmykitchen.com","note":"Mely Martínez's long-running blog of Mexican home-style recipes.","url":"https://www.mexicoinmykitchen.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Pati Jinich","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"patijinich.com","note":"Recipe archive from the Mexican-born chef and host of the PBS series Pati's Mexican Table.","url":"https://patijinich.com/recipes/","sugge